    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d293">

                <pb facs="0694" n="663" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v02/pg663"/>

                <head>293. Memorandum From President-Elect Nixon to Secretary of State-Designate
                        Rogers<anchor n="1" xml:id="d293-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1969-01-07">January 7, 1969</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>At the beginning of a new Administration I believe that an analysis of the
                    qualifications of all of our Ambassadors abroad, career as well <pb facs="0695"
                        n="664" xml:id="pg664"/> as non-career, should be made. While the great
                    majority of career men will probably be retained in their present posts, the
                    beginning of a new Administration is a good time to move some of the dead wood
                    out and to move some of the unqualified men from one post to a less sensitive
                    one.</p>
                <p>In my travels abroad I have, of course, seen the usual number of political
                    appointees who weren’t qualified for the job they held, but I have also seen a
                    number of career men who were pretty inadequate and who should be replaced.</p>
                <p>I think a very hard-headed analysis should be made just as soon as we take over
                    on January 20 so that any changes can be made within the first two or three
                    months that we are in office. If we delay beyond that point we will be subject
                    to the charge of being vindictive, personal or political. Changes at this time,
                    of course, will be expected.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
